\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par The topic of this session is migration from ASP (Active Server Pages) to PHP.  Although ASP can use multiple languages (anything from JavaScript to PHP), the language most commonly used and the language we will focus on is VBScript.  Also, at the time thi
s document was written, PHP4 was still in beta (beta 3).  Since PHP4 has some dramatic improvements over PHP3, it will be the version this document references. 
\par 
\par If you currently have a large Web site designed using ASP that you want to move to PHP, there i
s no simple way of doing it without a lot of hard work.  The bigger the Web site, the more work it will be.  If you are debating to switch from ASP to PHP, or are making a decision on what to build your next Internet application with, the sooner you decid
e, the better.  I hope this presentation will introduce you to PHP and give you enough information to make an informed decision.
\par 
\par With this said, there is one application that is dedicated to converting ASP pages to PHP. This application is called asp2php}{\cs18\f1\fs22\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s17\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs18\super \chftn }{ http://home.i1.net/~naken/asp2PHP/}}}{\f1\fs22 
. Although I have not tested it extensively, it did do a fairly good job at converting a very simple ASP page to PHP. However, I did have problems with more complex pages involving databases. The developer of this applic
ation, Michael Kohn, says in his FAQ that it can convert database applications written in ASP to PHP.  However, when I converted a fairly simple ASP page that retrieved quotes from a database, the asp2php application completely mangled my script.  This wa
s however the first time I ran the application so I may have not done something right.  At any rate, I couldn\rquote t even find my SQL statement.  
\par 
\par I am also currently in the process of developing a database that will allow you to type in a VBScript command and 
get the PHP equivalent. Ironically, the initial version of this application will be written in ASP. I will also create an exact duplicate using PHP and make the source code publicly available for both systems in order to compare. This database will be ava
ilable at: 
\par 
\par http://www.periodically.com/apps/asp_to_php/
\par 
\par The location of the PHP version will be noted at this location as well when it is complete.  I suspect it will be located somewhere on the Covalent Web site.
\par }{\b\f1\fs22 
\par }{\f1\fs22 Applications like asp2php may help conv
ert some of your pages from ASP to PHP but there is no better tool for the job at hand like the human mind. Therefore, the rest of this session will be dedicated to showing you some differences and similarities between ASP and PHP.  This session is intend
ed to give you a better understanding of the PHP language to help you make the move from ASP to PHP.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel0\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f28\fs36\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f1\fs22 
\par }\pard \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel0\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f1\fs22 \page Open Source
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
One of the biggest differences between Active Server Pages and PHP is that PHP is open source software. As most people in the computer indu
stry know, open source software means that the source code to this software is free to the public (still, read the license agreement). Programmers can read, redistribute, modify and even sell open source software to or for whomever they want, however they
 want.  This model has several advantages over the traditional closed software model.  
\par 
\par The first advantage is flexibility.  I\rquote ll give an example. In PHP, to denote code that is to be processed on the server, it is surrounded by special begin tags and end tags.
\par 
\par <?PHP echo \'93Hello World\'94 ?>
\par 
\par In version 3.0.4, to accommodate people who are used to writing ASP code, they added the ASP-style tags.  Now, if the uses of ASP-style tags are enabled in the configuration file, the above code can be written as:
\par 
\par <% echo \'93Hello World\'94 %>
\par 
\par or, adding another shortcut from ASP, 
\par 
\par <%= \'93Hello World\'94 %>
\par  
\par Every one of these different styles will do the same thing; they will all print \'93Hello World\'94
 on the Web page. In a closed source environment, innovations such as these are much less likely to happen.  The manufacturer of closed source software has many reasons not to acknowledge the existence of anything but their product.  Doing so would give c
redit to the competition and may detract from sales.
\par 
\par A second advantage of open source software is reliability.  Since the source code is open to the public, programmers and non-programmers are able to download the program at any given time in its developm
ent and test it.  People from around the world give feedback on what works and what doesn\rquote t work.  A far greater number of people are able to test software before it is released.  This means that many more bugs are found and fixed.
\par 
\par Often times, the people who download this code are interested in contributing.  They not only find bugs, but also fix them.  If a new feature is thought of that will contribute to the functionality of the software, it can be added at any time by anyone. 

\par 
\par In a closed source envi
ronment, code is fiercely guarded as intellectual property.  The only people who work on a given program are the people hired to do so and they sign agreements not to disclose anything they create for the company.  Due to the limited amount of money a com
pany has, only limited amounts of problems can be found and fixed.  Often times, programs are released well before they are completed in order to make it to market as fast as possible.  These releases are generally followed by \'93fixes\'94
.  When bugs are found, the users of this software must wait until a fix is released or work around the problem. 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Portability
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par As Covalent Technologies Web developer, one of my responsibilities is to help build and maintain the company intranet. When I first got to Covalent, o
ne of my initial tasks was to redesign the navigation system for our intranet.  The old version used frames as a navigation bar.  Whenever someone hit reload, the browser would take the user to the home page. As more and more features were added, things b
ecame more crowded.
\par 
\par The solution we decided to use was to create a dropdown menu system using DHTML.  An example of this DHTML menu can be found at }{\field{\*\fldinst {\f1\fs22  HYPERLINK "http://www.periodically.com" }{\f1\fs22 {\*\datafield 
00d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b02000000170000001c00000068007400740070003a002f002f007700770077002e0070006500720069006f0064006900630061006c006c0079002e0063006f006d000000e0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b3a00000068007400740070003a002f002f007700770077002e00
70006500720069006f0064006900630061006c006c0079002e0063006f006d002f00000000}}}{\fldrslt {\cs16\f1\fs22\ul\cf2 http://www.periodically.com}}}{\f1\fs22  (IE 4+ only). Since Microsoft Internet Explorer\rquote 
s implementation of DHTML, JavaScript, HTML and Cascading Style Sheets is by far better than any other browser, we chose this as the standard.  Once completed, everything worked great because everyone had a Windows platform and could run IE.
\par 
\par Soon however, as we started to hire more programmers, it became obvious that requiring Internet Explorer was not going to be an option.  Since Covalent writes software mainly for Unix/Linux platforms, many of the develop
ers run these as their primary operating systems.  Internet Explorer only runs on two flavors of Unix, the Macintosh, and of course Windows}{\cs18\f1\fs22\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain 
\s17\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs18\super \chftn }{ http://www.microsoft.com/windows/ie/download/all.htm?bShowPage}}}{\f1\fs22 
.  We had to revert to the old style of navigation 
for all users who were not running one of these operating systems and now support two versions of our Intranet.  Since the source code is not available to the public, only Microsoft can choose to compile their Web browser for different platforms.  Their W
eb browser is not portable.
\par 
\par Active Server Pages are the same way.  Unless you purchase an expensive ASP emulation package such as Chili!Soft ASP ($3,995 per processor for Solaris and IBM-AIX), you must run Windows NT.  If you build an extensive Web site us
ing this technology, you will not be able to change platforms without a lot of work. 
\par 
\par PHP on the other hand is open to the public.  If someone wants to run PHP on a Unix box or even on Windows, PHP can be compiled for that operating system.  Therefore, PHP is completely free and can run on almost all operating systems.
\par 
\par Another advantage is that PHP is not tied down to one Web server while ASP\rquote s can run only on IIS. PHP works with Apache, IIS, Zeus, Netscape, Personal Web Server, and any other server that supports CGI.
\par 
\par It also supports many native database adaptors. ASP supports MySQL and ODBC databases where PHP supports MySQL, MSQL, Oracle, Informix, ODBC, dBase as well as several other databases}{\cs18\f1\fs22\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain 
\s17\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs18\super \chftn }{ http://www.php.net/manual/intro-whatcando.php3}}}{\f1\fs22 
.  Since ODBC is required to communicate between the ASP page and the database, native database adaptors can offer much better performance.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\page Performance
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par In a recent article by Hotwired\rquote s Web Monkey}{\cs18\f1\fs22\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s17\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs18\super 
\chftn }{ http://hotwired.lycos.com/webmonkey/99/51/index3a_page2.html?tw=programming}}}{\f1\fs22 ,  PHP4 is compared to PHP3 as well as ASP.  Many of the comparisons are listed below and were initially released on the Zend.org Website.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Speed Comparisons}{\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 The comparisons here are between ASP, PHP3, PHP4, and the PHP optimizing comp
iler.  The PHP optimizing compiler is an add on to the Zend engine that you must pay for.  The optimizing compiler works by compiling a script automatically the first time it is run.  Subsequent executions of this script will then use the compiled version
 improving performance dramatically.
\par 
\par Take a look at the differences between PHP3, PHP4 and PHP4 using the optimizing compiler on a quicksort benchmark.  Sorting an array of 10 elements, PHP4 is 66 percent times faster than PHP3.  Increasing the array to 1,
000 elements, the performance increases dramatically to 460 percent increase.  Through in the optimizing compiler and you get over a 1000 percent increase.
\par 
\par There are a few other examples of speed differences, but lets take a look at an important benchmark
, retrieving rows from a database and rendering them as HTML. A benchmark of 1000 rows runs 525 percent faster using PHP4, 765 percent faster using the optimizing compiler. To better understand this increase in speed, a query that normally would take 4.25
 seconds in PHP3 will now only take about .83 seconds using the Zend powered PHP4, and only .55 seconds with the Zend optimizing compiler. These are speed increases that your users will notice.
\par 
\par So how does this compare to ASP?  The quicksort benchmark runs
 about 440 percent slower than ASP.  Add the compiler however, and PHP is 66 percent faster.  A simple loop turning over 10 million times is 2 percent faster than ASP.  Through in the compiler and you get a speed increase of 352 percent. I couldn\rquote 
t find any comparisons on the database rendering benchmark.
\par 
\par In all, PHP is very comparable in performance to ASP.  Throw in the compiler and PHP starts to out perform ASP.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\page }{\b\f1\fs22 Syntax
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f1\fs22 
\par }{\f1\fs22 PHP syntax is very much like JavaScript or C.  If you know one of these two languages, you will have no problem learning PHP. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Embedded Code}{\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
PHP, like Asp, is an embedded scripting language.  Any file with the extension PHP (filename.php) is run on the s
erver and the code is imbedded in the HTML.  An example of this was show under the Open Source comparison above.  In any HTML document, PHP code can be embedded as follows:
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <html>
\par <head>
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 <title>Example</title>
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 </head>
\par <body>
\par \tab <? echo \'93Hello World\'94; ?><br>
\par \tab <%= \'93Hello World\'94 ?>
\par </body>
\par </html>}{\b\f2\fs22 
\par }{\b\f1\fs22 
\par }{\f1\fs22 Anything within the <? ?> tags will be processed on the server and the results sent to the client as plain HTML.  In the above example, you will notice that there is similar embedded code using VBScript.  This is bec
ause there is an option in PHP to turn on ASP-Style tags.  Although it looks like ASP, it is actually PHP.
\par 
\par One other important note, in VBScript a new line is indicated by a line break.  In PHP, the end of a line is expressed using the semicolon (;).  One
-line statements like above do not require a semicolon but multi-line statements do.  If not, a parsing error will occur.  This difference is the most important concept to learn to understand syntax in PHP
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab echo \'93Hello World<br>\'94; 
\par \tab echo \'93without semi colons, an error would occur\'94;
\par ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par NOTE: For the rest of this session, PHP code will be indicated by the use of <? ?> tags and ASP code will be indicated by the use of <% %> tags
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par Printing Text}{\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 In VBScript, the Response Object sends output to the client. To
 output simple text, the write method is used (an equal sign [=] can be substituted as a short cut).
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 \tab <% response.write \'93Hello World\'94 %>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par In PHP, there are several different ways to print text. Echo, print and printf are all different ways to output text.  Echo and print are the closest to the write method. The following example is equivalent to the example above
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 \tab <? echo \'93Hello World\'94; ?>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Comments}{\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 Often tim
es when writing more complex code, it is important to add comments so you as well as others, can understand it at a later date.  In ASP, the single quote (\lquote ) is used to denote a comment.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab \lquote  the variable a is defined somewhere above.
\par \tab response.write a
\par %>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par In PHP, single quotes and double quotes are interchangeable.  Therefore, a combination of forward slashes is used to denote a comment.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab // the variable $a is defined somewhere above.
\par \tab echo $a;
\par ?>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par A second way to comment code in PHP is the used of /* */.  This method is usually used for larger sections of code.  
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab /* \tab use a forward slash and asterisk to denote the 
\par \tab \tab beginning of a large comment and an asterisk
\par \tab \tab and forward slash to denote the end.\tab */
\par ?>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Variables
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
Another important part of any language is variables. Variables in PHP and VBScript are quite different.  In VBScript, a variable can be declared using three different statements, Dim, Private and Public.  These statements all deal with scope.  Variables h
owever, do not have to be declared.  In VBScript, a variable can be any word that is not already used as a key word by VBScript.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab a = 1
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 b = 2
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \tab c = a + b
\par \tab response.write c
\par %>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par This will return a value of 3.
\par 
\par In PHP, variables are declared by using the dollar sign ($).  Without it, a parsing error will occur.  Variable scope can be defined using the static or global statements. 
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab $a = 1;
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 $b = 2;
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \tab $c = $a + $b;
\par \tab echo $c;
\par ?>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Variables and Strings}{\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 An important function of scripting languages is combining v
ariables and strings.  PHP and VBScript can do the same thing although PHP has created a shortcut.  PHP has the ability to put variables into strings.  Lets take a look.
\par }{\f2\fs22 
\par <?\tab $a = \'93Dogs\'94;
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 echo \'93$a are smart\'94;
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Notice that the variable ($a) is included within the quotes. This will return \'93Dogs are smart\'94.  This is because variables start with the dollar sign.  In VBScript, the scripting engine wouldn\rquote 
t be able to tell the difference between a variable and text if it both were in a string. Of course variables however don\rquote 
t have to be included within the quotes.  This brings us to another difference between PHP and VBScript; how to combine strings.
\par 
\par In VBScript, to combine two strings, the ampersand is used.  
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab a = \'93Dogs\'94
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 response.write a & \'93 are smart\'94;
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 %>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par In PHP, a period is used.
\par }{\f2\fs22 
\par <?\tab $a = \'93Dogs\'94;
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 echo $a . \'93 are smart\'94;
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par This is a minor difference but is something that comes up soon and is very important.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i0\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Request Object}{\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 One of the most important aspects of Web applicat
ions is the passing of information from one page to another.  This can be done through the use of forms, cookies, sessions or query strings.  In the ASP world, the way to obtain this data is by using the Request Object. The request object has four main co
llections; Cookies, Form, QueryString and ServerVariables.
\par 
\par Lets say on one page you have a form.  When a user fills out this form, you want to take the information the user entered and save it in a database. To do this, you must retrieve the data the user 
has submitted.  In ASP, this task is simple.  Anytime you want to retrieve data from an HTTP request, you use the request object.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab request.form(\'93Name\'94)
\par \tab request.querystring(\'93Name\'94)
\par \tab request.cookies(\'93Name\'94)
\par 
\par \tab request(\'93Name\'94)
\par %>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par If you don\rquote t specify a specific HTTP request to check, the request object will check all collections in the following order until it finds the first one. 
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f1\fs22 \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 1.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls12\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f1\fs22 QueryString
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f1\fs22 \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 2.\tab}Form
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f1\fs22 \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 3.\tab}Cookies
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f1\fs22 \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 4.\tab}ClientCertificates
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f1\fs22 \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.\tab}ServerVariables
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs22 
\par In PHP, this process is greatly simplified.  PHP takes away the power to check a specific HTTP request.  Instead, it checks them all.  Therefore, if one of the fields of a form was \'93Name\'94
, it will check all HTTP requests until it finds it.  In essence, all variables are treated the same.  Whether a variable (in this case Name) is coming from a session, cookie, form, querystring, or was set on that page, the syntax is as follows:
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab echo $name;\tab ?>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par Although this does make things a lot simpler, confusion can set in when trying to determine where variables are coming from.  When using the Request Object, it is easy to tell where the variable is coming from.  If it is request.form(\'93name\'94
), you know the variable has come from a form.  If it is request.querystring(\'93name\'94), the variable came from the querystring.  If it is simply request(\'93name\'94
), you still know it is coming from an HTTP request and was not a variable set on the page.  In PHP, variables and HTTP requests look the same.  That is why it is important t
o prefix your form variables to tell where certain values are coming from.  The following table is what I use to prefix variables from forms, querystrings, cookies and sessions (explained below).
