\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Statements(VBScript) / Control Structures(PHP)
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 Once you have
 a basic understanding of how variables and strings work, control structures become very important. Please note that the list of statements given here is not a complete list.  This demonstration is designed to familiarize you with the differences between 
the two languages to get you started.  For more advanced Statements, consult the PHP.net website.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\fs22 \page If\'85Then}{\f1\fs22  
\par This statement conditionally executes a group of statements, depending on the value of an expression. In VBScript, every }{\b\f1\fs22 If}{\f1\fs22  or }{\b\f1\fs22 ElseIf}{\f1\fs22  must end with a }{\b\f1\fs22 Then}{\f1\fs22 .  
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab If condition Then
\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [statements]
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [ElseIf condition-n Then
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [elseif statements]]
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [Else
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [else statements]]
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 End If
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 %>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par In PHP, the }{\b\f1\fs22 if}{\f1\fs22  construct functions similarly to VBScript except with a lot less syntax.  There is no }{\b\f1\fs22 Then}{\f1\fs22  or }{\b\f1\fs22 End If}{\f1\fs22  constructs.  For single statement }{\b\f1\fs22 if}{\f1\fs22 
 construct, all that is needed is the condition and the statement. The end of a statement is denoted with a semicolon. This is the basic difference between the two languages.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab if(condition) 
\par \tab \tab [statement];
\par ?> 
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par For multiple statement }{\b\f1\fs22 if}{\f1\fs22  constructs, brackets are used to contain several different statements in one statement group.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab if(condition) \{
\par \tab \tab [statement one];
\par \tab \tab [statement two];
\par \tab \}
\par ?> 
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Also important to the }{\b\f1\fs22 if}{\f1\fs22  control structure is the }{\b\f1\fs22 else}{\f1\fs22  and }{\b\f1\fs22 elseif}{\f1\fs22 
 constructs.  These behave the same as in VBScript except the syntax follows the same pattern as above.  Multiple statements are held in a statement group by brackets. 
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab if(condition) \{
\par \tab \tab [statement one];
\par \tab \tab [statement two];
\par \tab \}
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 elseif (condition) \{
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \tab \tab [statement three];
\par \tab \tab [statement four];
\par \tab \}
\par \tab else \{
\par \tab \tab [statement five];
\par \tab \}
\par ?> 
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Again, statements end with a semicolon.  Without a semicolon at the end, a parse error will occur.}{\b\f1\fs22 \page For\'85Next}{\f1\fs22  
\par The }{\b\f1\fs22 For\'85Next}{\f1\fs22  statement repeats a group of statements a specified number of times.  The differences between the VBScript and PHP versions of this control structure are a little more different than the }{\b\f1\fs22 If\'85Then}{
\f1\fs22  statement.
\par 
\par In VBScript, the syntax is as follows:
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab For counter = start To end (Step step)
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [statements]
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 Exit For
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [statements]
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 Next
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 
\par \tab For a = 1 To 10\tab \tab \lquote  practical example 
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 If a = 8 Then
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\f2\fs22 Exit For
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \tab \tab End If
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 b = b + a
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 Next
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 
\par %>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Here, a variable is used to keep track of how many loops have been repeated, and the number of loops to repeat is indicated by a }{\i\f1\fs22 start}{\f1\fs22  and }{\i\f1\fs22 end}{\f1\fs22  part.  }{\b\f1\fs22 Step}{\f1\fs22 
 can be used to specify the amount the counter is changed each time through the loop. }{\b\f1\fs22 Exit For}{\f1\fs22  can be used to stop the }{\b\f1\fs22 For\'85Next}{\f1\fs22  statement if conditions are met, and }{\b\f1\fs22 Next}{\f1\fs22 
 indicates the end of the statement.
\par 
\par In PHP, the syntax is quite different. Again, brackets are used to contain multiple statements, but note the structure of the }{\b\f1\fs22 for}{\f1\fs22  expression.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab for (expr1; expr2; expr3) \{
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [statements];
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \tab break;
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 [statements];
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \tab \}
\par ?>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par At the beginning of the loop, expression 1 is evaluated unconditionally and only onc
e.  Expression 2 is evaluated at the beginning of every iteration.  The loop will continue until this expression is false.  Expression 3 is evaluated at the end of every iteration. The }{\b\f1\fs22 break}{\f1\fs22 
 statement can be used to end the loop prematurely.  The following example will do the same as the second ASP example above.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab for ($a = 0; $a <= 8; $a++) \{\tab // practical example
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 if($a == 8)\{
