\par }{\b\f1\fs22 Exit Do}{\f1\fs22  in this case will exit the loop prematurely.
\par 
\par In PHP, the equivalent statement is the }{\b\f1\fs22 while}{\f1\fs22  statement.  }{\b\f1\fs22 While}{\f1\fs22  statements are the simplest forms of loop in PHP.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab while (condition) \{
\par \tab \tab [statements];
\par \tab break;
\par \tab \tab [statements];
\par \tab \}
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 $i = 1; \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab // practical example
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \tab while ($i <= 10) \{
\par \tab \tab if($i == 8) \{break;\}
\par      \tab \tab echo $i++;
\par \tab \}
\par ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par In the practical example above, the variable $i will print eight times.
\par 
\par These three Control Structures are some of the basic statements used in PHP and ASP.  The reason for showing these is not to give a how to lesson in PHP or VBScript, but rather to show a basic differences between the syntax.  Where VBScript uses words at 
the beginning }{\i\f1\fs22 and}{\f1\fs22 
 end to contain statements within the control structure, PHP uses brackets to contain statements within the control structure. Also, after every statement, PHP requires a semicolon to denote the end of a line where in VBScript, a line break
 denotes the end of a line.
\par 
\par With these basic syntax differences in mind, learning the rest of the control structures should not be difficult.
\par 
\par NOTE: Although in the above examples I did not mention it, there is alternative syntax for people who like the syntax of VBScript.  This alternate syntax can be found at the PHP.net Web site.  A simple example is the following }{\b\f1\fs22 If..Then}{
\f1\fs22  statement:
