\par }{\b\f1\fs22 Functions and Sub Routines
\par }{\f1\fs22 In VBScript, a Sub Routine is a procedure that takes a list of arguments, performs a series of statements, and changes the values of the arguments.  The difference between a sub statement and a function statement is sub statement can\rquote 
t be used as an expression.  In PHP, there are no sub routines, only functions. 
\par 
\par In VBScript as well as PHP, a function is a procedure that takes a list of arguments, performs a series of statements, and changes the values of the arguments. The function can then return a value or return nothing. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
The basic difference between a function in VBScript and a function in PHP is of course the style of syntax (VBScript using words to begin and end containment of statements) and the way a value is returned.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par Here is an example of a function in VBScript.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab Function name(arg1, arg2... argn)
\par \tab \tab [statements]
\par \tab \tab [name = expression]
\par \tab Exit Function
\par \tab \tab [statements]
\par \tab \tab [name = expression]
\par \tab End Function
\par %>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par In VBScript, the way to return a value is to set the name equal to an expression (value).  So if you wanted to use a function as an expression in VBScript, you would do the following:
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab Function figureCost(quantity, partNumber)
\par \tab \tab part = \'93002-a0032\'94
\par \tab \tab If partNumber = part Then
\par \tab \tab \tab figureCost = (quantity * 500)
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 End if
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \tab End Function
\par 
\par \tab If figureCost(30, \'93002-a0032\'94) = 15000 Then
\par \tab \tab Response.Write \'93You bought 30 parts\'94
\par \tab Else\tab 
\par \tab \tab Response.Write \'93You didn\rquote t buy 30 parts\'94
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 End if
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 %>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par Although this example is very simple and makes little sense, it shows several things.  First, it shows how to use the function figureCost as an expression in an }{\b\f1\fs22 if\'85then}{\f1\fs22 
 statement. It also shows that in order to return a value, the value to be returned must be set equal to the name of the function (}{\f2\fs22 figureCost = (quantity * 500)}{\f1\fs22 ).
\par 
\par PHP uses a much more intuitive system to return a value.  It uses the optional }{\b\f1\fs22 return}{\f1\fs22  statement. Below is the syntax for the PHP function:
\par }{\f2\fs22 
\par <?\tab function name(arg1, arg2... argn)\{
\par \tab \tab [statements];
\par \tab \tab return $value;
\par \tab \}
\par ?>}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par The functionality in PHP is very similar, the syntax is just slightly different.  One thing to note; PHP functions can do something that VBScript can\rquote t which is to have a function within a function. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i0\fs22 
