One thing to note that is a little confusing to some people is setting a variable equa
l to a value verses comparing values.  Using a single equal sign (=) sets a variable equal to a value. Using the double equal sign (==) compares two strings.  If a function or statement isn\rquote t behaving properly, this is a common mistake to look for.

\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab $a = 10; ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Using two equal signs compares two values.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab If($a == $b)\{
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f2\fs22 echo \'93a is the same as b\'94;
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 \}
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22  
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f1\fs22 Sessions
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs22 
\par One advantage of ASP over PHP is the Session Event.  In ASP, a session is created when a person opens a Web page.  Sessions use cookies to k
eep track of a person and variables associated with that person.  In ASP, application variables using the Application Event can also be created.  These variable are available to all users, all the time. PHP does not support application variables however i
n PHP4, session variables are supported.
\par 
\par In VBScript, a session variable is set using the following syntax:
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab Session(\'93Name\'94) = \'93Craig\'94\tab 
\par \tab Session(\'93Age\'94) = \'9328\'94
\par %>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par As long as the user does not close his browser or the session.timeout value does not expire, these values can be retrieved by any page within the application.  The syntax used to retrieve these values is:
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab response.write Session(\'93Name\'94)
\par \tab response.write Session(\'93Age\'94)
\par %>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Before PHP4, the only way to do this was with a somewhat complex librar
y for PHP called PHPLib.  By including a variety of files on the top of each page, session variables could be created and stored in a database.  This was a lot of extra overhead and took a lot of configuring.  In PHP4 however, the session event is compile
d into the Zend engine.  This increases performance and decreases hassle.
\par 
\par In PHP4, setting a session variable still isn\rquote t quite the no-brainer as the examples above, but it is a lot simpler than in PHP3.  
\par 
\par In PHP4, sessions are not automatically initializ
ed.  There is either a setting in the PHP.ini file that will let you turn on sessions for all Web pages, or you can use the session_start() function at the top of every page you want to use a session for.  Using the function setting can decrease overhead.
 When a session variable is not needed on a particular page, the session_start function need not be called. 
\par 
\par Once sessions are enabled for PHP4, you can then start creating session variables.  To create a session variable, the variable name must first be registered.  Once registered, a value can be assigned to that variable as if it were a normal variable.

\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab session_register(\'93sn_name\'94); 
\par \tab $sn_name = \'93Craig\'94;
\par ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Once registered, this variable is used like all other variables.  To write this variable to a Web page, the following code would be used:
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab echo $sn_name; ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Since once a session variable is registered in PHP, it is handled just like any other variable,
 it is smart to put some sort of prefix in front of it so you can tell that it is in fact a session variable.  In the example above, I used the prefix sn_.  You can use whatever you want.
\par 
\par If you want to remove a session variable from memory, in ASP you simply set the variable equal to null.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <%\tab Session(\'93Name\'94) = NULL
\par \tab Session(\'93Age\'94) = NULL
\par %>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par In PHP, you unregister the variable.  Make sure that you don\rquote t include the dollar sign ($) in the variable name.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <?\tab Session_unregister(\'93sn_name\'94); 
\par \tab Session_unregister(\'93sn_age\'94);
\par ?>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par These differences aren\rquote t better or worse in my opinion, just different.  With a simple prefix, telling the difference between a session variable and normal variable is no harder than in ASP.
\par 
\par The PHP sessions do have some features missin
g that ASP has that are valuable.  One of these features is the Session.Timeout property.  In ASP, you can set the timeout of a session on the Web page.  This is useful if you want to specify per Web application or even by person, the session timeout valu
e
.  In PHP, this value must be set in the PHP.ini configuration file.  This means once set, it is set for all Web applications.  A file can be written to override this value on an application level basis or even folder basis, but this is kind of a hassle. 
 This is fairly minor but the more control you have in the code, the better.
\par 
\par The feature that I miss the most in PHP however is the global.asa file.  According to Microsoft documentation}{\cs18\f1\fs22\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s17\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs18\super \chftn }{ http://msdn.microsoft.com/isapi/msdnlib2.idc?theURL=/library/psdk/iisref/glob63vp.htm}}}{\f1\fs22 , \'93
The Global.asa file is an optional file in which you can specify event scripts and declare objects that have session or application scope. It is not a content file displayed to the users; instead it stores event information and object
s used globally by the application.\'94
\par 
\par The global.asa file can be set up to create scripts and set session variables, all in one file.  When a new session is created, the scripts in this file are run.  The alternative in PHP is to create a file with all you
r scripts and include it (using an include statement or the Web server) to the top of every page or auto-prepend the script to all pages.  If\'85
then statements have to be written to check each time if the session variables have already been set, but it will 
still work.  The global.asa file to me seems like a much cleaner way of creating session wide variables when a session is first created by the browser.
\par  
\par In any event, session variables are a very important part of server side scripting languages and will be included with PHP4.
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Database Connectivity
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par Probably the most important part of any scripting language is its ability to connect to a database.  As the Web becomes more of a plac
e for ecommerce and advanced Web applications, efficiently storing and retrieving data from a database becomes more and more important.  
\par 
\par This next part will deal with the differences between ASP connections to an ODBC database and PHP connections to a MySQL database.  
\par 
\par Microsoft\rquote s ASP can connect to any database using the ActiveX Data Objects (ADO). Microsoft\rquote s definition of the ADO is: \'93
Designed to replace the need for all other high-level data access methods, ADO can access relational, Indexed Sequential Access Method (ISAM), or hierarchical databases, or any type of data source\emdash as long as there is an ODBC-compliant driver.\'94

\par 
\par PHP uses a collection of functions to connect and manipulate the data in a database.
\par 
\par The best way to compare the ASP ADO model and the PHP connections is to give an example.  I will use a page based on my personal Web site that gets random quotes from a database and renders it to HTML}{\cs18\f1\fs22\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain 
\s17\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs18\super \chftn }{ http://www.periodically.com/library/quotes/}}}{\f1\fs22 .
\par 
\par Lets begin.  The full example of the ASP and PHP versions can be found at the end of this document.
\par \page First, since this is a random quote generator, the total number of records in the database table must be determined.  Once determined, a random number between 1 and the number of records must be created. 
 In the ASP example, at the top of the page, you will see the following:
\par }{\f2\fs28 
\par }{\f2\fs22 <% randomize %>
\par <!--#include file="../../data/adovbs.asp"-->
\par <html>
\par <head>
\par \tab <title>quotes about beer</title>
\par </head>
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs22 
Randomize is a statement in VBScript that initializes the random-number generator. In PHP, initialization is not necessary.  There is no equivalent to this statement.  The next line is an include file containing several constants necessary for ADO.  This 
also is not necessary in PHP.
\par  
\par To find the number of records in the database table, we first make a connection to the database.
\par 
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 1}{\f2\fs18 \tab Set dbQuotes = Server.CreateObject("ADODB.Connection")
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 2}{\f2\fs18 \tab dbQuotes.Open "craig", "(username)", "(password)" 
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Using VBScript, on line 1, we create a Connection Object giving it a variable name.  In this case the variable name is dbQuotes.  On line 2, we open the Connection Object using the Open method.  Variables for the Open method include the data source name 
\'93craig\'94, a username and password.  The data source name is a reference to the database as well as its location. The Username and password are optional depending on the database you are using and it\rquote s security.
\par 
\par In PHP, a connection to the database is similar.  
\par 
\par }{\fs18 1\tab $dbQuotes =  mysql_connect}{\f2\fs18 (\'93(host)\'94, \'93(username)\'94,}{ }{\f2\fs18 \'93(password)\'94);
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par $dbQuotes is the variable name that represents the connection to the database.  Variables include the location of the MySQL server as well as an optional username and password.  The 
main difference is that an object is not created.  Instead, a connect function is used.  Also, the host variable does not tell us which database to use; it only gives the location of the MySQL database.  
\par 
\par Back to ASP.  Next, we use the Connection object we just created to obtain data from the table.  In VBScript, we create a recordset object.
\par 
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 3}{\f2\fs18 \tab Set rstGetQuote = Server.CreateObject("ADODB.Recordset")
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 4}{\f2\fs18 \tab rstGetQuote.ActiveConnection = dbQuotes
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 5}{\f2\fs18 \tab rstGetQuote.CursorType = adOpenStatic
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 6}{\f2\fs18 \tab rstGetQuote.LockType = adLockOptimistic
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs22 Line 3, we create the Recordset Object giving it a variable name of rstGetQuote.  In line 4, we tell the object which connection to use.  Line 5 and 6 set the cursor and lock type.  In PHP, it is not necessary to worry about line 5 and 6
 because it is already taken care of. 
\par 
\par We continue with our ASP example by obtaining how many records are in our quote table. In line 7 we tell the object we created above to use the table \'93beerquotes\'94 as the source of our query.
\par 
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 7}{\f2\fs18 \tab rstGetQuote.Source = "beerquotes"
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 8}{\f2\fs18 \tab rstGetQuote.Open 
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 9}{\f2\fs18 \tab \tab intGetQuoteCount = rstGetQuote.RecordCount
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 10}{\f2\fs18 \tab rstGetQuote.Close
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par In line 8, we open a connection to the recordset object. In line 9 we set the variable intGetQuoteCount equal to the number or records in the \'93beerquotes\'94 table.  Line 10, we close the connection.
\par 
\par In PHP, these eight lines can be reduced down to three because of the use of functions.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs18 2\tab $sql = "SELECT quotes, author FROM beerquotes";
\par 3\tab $qryQuotes = mysql_db_query(\'93dbCraig\'94, $sql, $dbQuotes);
\par 4\tab $intGetQuoteCount = mysql_num_rows($qryQuotes);
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Line 2 is a simple SQL statement that references the table.  In line 3, we set the variable $qryQuotes equal to a MySQL function that runs the query.  
\par 
\par Note here that the variable name is not the same as th
e ASP example ($rstGetQuote).  PHP requires an extra step (mysql_fetch_array() (below)) once the query is run to get the data into an array where we can obtain the values. Here, we are only counting the number of rows in the table.  
\par 
\par Line 4 uses the mysql_num_rows() function to find how many rows are in this recordset.
\par 
\par So far we have show how to open a connection to a database in both languages.  ADO is an object-based model while PHP is a function-based model.  We now have the number of rows in the table.
\par 
\par The next step is to generate a random number to determine the quote to get.  In VBScript, we use the Rnd function.  This function generates a random number.
\par 
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 11}{\f2\fs18 \tab intIDDisplay = Int((intGetQuoteCount - 1 + 1) * Rnd + 1)
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par We assign this number to the variable intIDDisplay.  Notice that this method is somewhat complicated.  Anyway, here is the syntax for it.  
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs22 
\par Int((upperbound - lowerbound + 1) * Rnd + lowerbound)
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs22 
\par In PHP, we use the rand() function.  This function is far simpler to use.  For one, we don\rquote t have to use an initialization statement.   The syntax is rand(}{\i\f1\fs22 lower value}{\f1\fs22 , }{\i\f1\fs22 upper value}{\f1\fs22 ).
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs18 5\tab $intIDDisplay = rand(1, $intGetQuoteCount);}{\f1\fs22 
\par 
\par This simple statement gets us the same value as above.
\par 
\par The final step is to get the data we need from the database.  In ASP, this requires using the same recordset object we previously closed.  But this time, we use an SQL statement with the Open method and drop the Source method.
\par 
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 12}{\f2\fs18 \tab rstGetQuote.Open "SELECT Author, Quote FROM beerQuotes WHERE ID = " & intIDDisplay
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 13}{\f2\fs18 \tab If rstGetQuote.BOF or rstGetQuote.EOF Then
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 14}{\f2\fs18 \tab \tab rstGetQuote.Close
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 15}{\f2\fs18 \tab \tab strQuote = \'93no quotes available\'94
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 16}{\f2\fs18 \tab Else
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 17}{\f2\fs18 \tab \tab strQuote = rstGetQuote("Quote")
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 18}{\f2\fs18 \tab \tab strAuthor = rstGetQuote("Author")
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 19}{\f2\fs18 \tab \tab rstGetQuote.Close
\par }{\b\f2\fs18 20}{\f2\fs18 \tab End If
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs22 Line 12 is where we open our recordset object with our SQL statement.  The ID filed in the database here is sequential (1, 2, 3\'85 n). On line 13, we have some basic error checking.  If when we execute the select statement, it comes up 
with no results, we then close the object and set the quote string equal to \'93no quotes available.\'94  If the ID field is not sequential and a record is looked up that doesn\rquote 
t exist, this is the text that will appear.  This is to help avoid errors messages appearing on the Web page.
\par 
\par If records do exist, the statement sets the two variables strQuote and strAuthor equal to the values in the database for that record.
\par 
\par In PHP, we can do the same thing using functions.  A quick note, notice how the variable $intIDDisplay is included within the quotes.  This is a subtle advantage over VBScript that makes things a little easier.
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs18 6\tab $sql = \'93SELECT author, quote FROM beerquotes WHERE ID = $intIDDisplay\'94;
\par 7\tab $qryGetQuote = mysql_db_query(\'93dbCraig\'94, $sql, $dbQuotes);
\par 8\tab if(mysql_num_rows($qryGetQuote) == 0) \{
\par 9\tab \tab $strQuote = \'93no quotes available\'94;
\par 10\tab \}
\par 11\tab else \{
\par 12\tab \tab $rstGetQuote = mysql_fetch_array($qryGetQuote);
\par 13\tab \tab $strQuote = $rstGetQuote[\'93quote\'94];
\par 14\tab \tab $strAuthor = $rstGetQuote[\'93author\'94];
\par 15\tab \}
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
Line 7 again is using the mysql_db_query() function.  The data is not yet in a form in which we can use.  In line 8 we use the mysql_num_rows() function again, this time as an expression.  This is in place of the BOF and EOF properties. If the results of 
this function are equal to zero, we set the $strQuote variable equal to \'93no quotes available\'94.  If the results of the function aren\rquote t equal to zero, we then set the two variables equal to the values in the database.  
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par Notice that the only difference between getting the value from the MySQL array and the ADO recordset object is brackets vs. parentheses.
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs22 $rstGetQuote[\'93quote\'94] vs. rstGetQuote(\'93quote\'94)}{\f1\fs22 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs22 
\par After you have the values, print the values to the screen:
\par 
\par }{\f2\fs22 \tab \'93<? echo $strQuote; ?>\'94 <br><br> - <? echo $strAuthor; ?>
\par 
\par or using ASP:
\par 
\par \tab \'93<%= strQuote %>\'94 <br><br> - <%= strAuthor %>
\par }{\f1\fs22 
\par Although there are more efficient ways of obtaining this data (such as using only one sql statement that gets all records, counting the records, them moving to one record randomly), this example allows for a little more demonstration.  
\par 
\par There is a lot of difference in the way database manipulation is handled but the logic is always the same.  Learning PHP is simply a matter of experience and memorization.  Being able to research pro
blems and find solutions through reading documentation is essential.
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f1\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Conclusion
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par PHP and ASP are both very powerful scripting languages.  Deciding on which to use depends on your experience and project requirements.  Once you have the logic of any progra
